@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — two protocols on one sentence
#
# The sixth section of the roll written for Za-Walda Hawaryat states the same
# protection twice in a single sentence. Spoken, the spear is turned back and
# nothing is added. Worn, the names deliver from the enemy and the wearer
# escapes by the will of God. Two protocols stand here rather than one,
# because the two halves reach different verdicts, and a single protocol over
# the sentence would have to average them into a verdict neither half carries.
#
# This is the case the note in examinations/carried-booklet-protection.ttl
# asked for. There the European booklet guarantees the bearer against drowning,
# burning, dying without the sacrament and an unjust sentence, and takes no
# escape; the note observes that the same corpus takes one elsewhere. Here one
# sentence of a different transmission does both, so the escape and its absence
# can be compared without comparing two traditions.
#

pmp:EthiopianSpokenNamesSpearExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the spoken names turn a spear back"@en, "Ob die gesprochenen Namen einen Speer zurückwenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that if a man wishes to thrust a spear into the speaker and the speaker repeats the names, the spear shall be turned back. It is examinable because the claim names a single public event at a single moment and attaches no condition to it beyond the speaking."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:EthiopianSpokenAsmatYieldsTurnedSpear ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One man who repeated the names as the thrust came and was struck by the spear. The wording admits no degree, names no further condition, and reserves nothing, so a single such case refutes the claim as it stands. A weaker form, that speakers are struck less often than others, would require the speakers to be counted before the outcomes are known, and no such count exists for any transmission in this holding." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ethiopian-orthodox-magic.ttl" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-07" ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected in either direction, and the obstacle is a property of the record: the men who carried these rolls are not a recorded set, and neither the deaths nor the survivals of the eighteenth-century owners of Ethiopian parchment rolls were written down anywhere the claim could be checked against. Refutable in principle, unattended in fact, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised."@en ;
    skos:note "The escape the wording does not take is the point of entering this at all. Nothing here says the spear returns only if the speaker is worthy, only if he believes, or only if God wills it — and the second half of the same sentence says exactly the last of those about the other form. The bare half was not written by an author who had no such clause available."@en ;
    skos:note "One escape is nevertheless built into the object rather than into the sentence, and a protocol has to meet it: every prayer of every roll read names the person it was written for. A bearer who was struck can always be said to have carried a roll made out to somebody else. That is recorded at pmc:EthiopianOwnerNamedInEveryPrayer and it is a condition of the object, not of the claim."@en .

pmp:EthiopianWornNamesSpearExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the worn names exclude anything"@en, "Ob die getragenen Namen etwas ausschließen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the names worn upon the breast shall deliver the wearer from his enemy and that he shall escape by the will of God. The verdict is reached from the wording and not from cases: the closing clause covers the outcome and its opposite."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:EthiopianWornAsmatYieldsDeliverance ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "None is available from the wording as it stands, and that is the finding rather than an omission of this protocol. Were the deliverance stated without the closing clause, one wearer struck while carrying the names would refute it, exactly as in the spoken form beside it. With the clause in place there is no outcome to look for." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A wearer of the names who escapes his enemy, and a wearer of the names who is struck down by him. The first is deliverance by the will of God; the second is a deliverance that was not willed. The sentence covers both and forbids neither, so no observation of a wearer's fate can bear on it." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ethiopian-orthodox-magic.ttl" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-07" ;
    skos:note "The state is not a milder version of the one beside it and does not report a weaker claim. The spoken form says something and no cases have been gathered; the worn form gathers nothing because there is nothing that would count. The difference is three words at the end of one sentence."@en ;
    skos:note "A falsifying condition had to be entered here saying that there is none. pm:FalsifierRequiredShape exempts only pm:noProcedureDevised, and pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate is the one verdict whose content is that no falsifier exists; the shape therefore demands of it exactly what it reports as absent. Recorded rather than worked around, and filed as its own finding."@en .
